+
+#### Force-layout determinism (template)
+
+Force simulations can be non-deterministic unless you control initialization and runtime.
+
+If a force layout is required:
+- fix initial positions deterministically (sorted nodes placed by a formula),
+- fix forces and strengths explicitly,
+- run **exactly N ticks** before first render.
+
+Reusable template:
+```js
+// 1) Stable ordering
+const nodes = data.slice().sort((a, b) => d3.ascending(a.id, b.id));
+
+// 2) Deterministic initial positions (grid around center)
+const cols = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(nodes.length));
+const spacing = 12; // choose relative to typical radii
+nodes.forEach((d, i) => {
+  const r = Math.floor(i / cols);
+  const c = i % cols;
+  d.x = width / 2 + (c - cols / 2) * spacing;
+  d.y = height / 2 + (r - cols / 2) * spacing;
+});
+
+// 3) Build simulation with explicit forces
+const simulation = d3.forceSimulation(nodes)
+  .force('center', d3.forceCenter(width / 2, height / 2))
+  .force('x', d3.forceX(d => clusterX[d.group]).strength(0.25))
+  .force('y', d3.forceY(d => clusterY[d.group]).strength(0.25))
+  .force('collide', d3.forceCollide(d => d.r + 1));
+
+// 4) Fixed tick run
+simulation.stop();
+for (let i = 0; i < 250; i++) simulation.tick();
+
+// 5) Render once using d.x/d.y
+node.attr('transform', d => `translate(${d.x},${d.y})`);
+```
+
+If you must keep the simulation running for interaction, still initialize positions deterministically and keep the same forces/strengths; avoid transitions.
 
